WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.100
Speaker 1: All right, let's jump right in
today.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.100
Many topics related to the pandemic that we'll
be talking about.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.510
We'll be talking about an anti vaccine hearing.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:09.350
We will be talking about lab leak.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:13.190
But first, let's just kind of reset where
we are.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:18.180
Coronavirus cases in the United States are
now down more than 94 percent.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:25.689
We are now at the point where we are asking,
with so many people refusing to get vaccinated,

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:30.359
how much lower a case is going to go because
this thing is going to keep floating around.

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:36.530
Are we getting to about as low a point in
daily cases as we're going to get any time

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:41.530
soon because the virus is going to keep floating
around among unvaccinated populations?

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.449
I think the answer is not quite yet.

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:47.430
I think we'll still see a decline, but we'll
know within two to three weeks.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:53.230
But we are down more than 94 percent from
the case peak in roughly mid-January.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:59.910
Now, in terms of U.S. coronavirus deaths,
we are now down more than 88 percent in daily

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:00.950
deaths.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:04.559
Still 400 people a day are dying in the United
States of coronavirus.

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:06.190
It's still a horrible number.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:13.000
But when we talk about getting back to baseline
risk, I believe it's around 300 people a day

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.480
over a year that during the average flu season
die in the United States.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:24.320
So we're getting very close to that baseline
level of risk that we see equivalent to the

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.810
flu.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.550
Four hundred is still high, but it is the
lowest it's been during the the entire pandemic.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:34.180
You've got to go back to March of 2020 to
find death numbers this low in the United

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.180
States.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.799
Now, there are many people saying and if you
emailed me, you know, David, is this actually

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:41.720
because of the vaccines or is this just because
of the seasonality?

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.290
Because if you look at cases and deaths last
summer, they were just as low and that's not

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.290
at all true.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.509
So the first question is, are the premises
right?

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:51.509
And they are not.

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:55.130
We are now at 14000 cases per day and declining.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.469
The lowest point last summer was 22000 cases
per day.

00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:02.539
And they went all the way up to 70000 cases
per day.

00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:04.270
So it's not even close.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:09.020
It's not true that cases were this low last
summer.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:13.670
Now, looking globally, cases are now down
about 50 percent.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.540
Last week, we were down 40 percent from the
global peak.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.320
Now we're down 50 percent from the global
peak.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:25.120
Remember that the global peak was about 830000
cases per day, an insane number.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.090
So this is already major progress, although
there's still a long way to go in.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.840
Many parts of the world are completely or
almost completely unvaccinated.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:41.080
And then globally deaths, which, as we now
know, lag cases by several weeks.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.890
Global deaths are down about 34 percent from
their peak.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:51.990
We still have nearly 10000 people a day dying
of covid globally.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:57.450
Much of that is the unvaccinated population,
but we are down from more than fourteen thousand

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:58.810
deaths a day.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.110
So everything's moving in the right direction.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.230
Never as quickly as we would like.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:08.700
But fortunately, vaccinations in the United
States are starting to tick back up again

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.570
after months long decline.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.760
So we started seeing this in mid-April.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.130
Daily vaccinations peaked at roughly three
point three million a day.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.260
That was a very good number.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.940
We were hoping to get the three point five
or even four million a day.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:28.550
But then vaccination started to decline and
decline and decline all the way down to under

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.990
one million vaccinations per day.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.510
Over the last week we were down.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:39.920
Last week, we had a one week average of 930
